  "preamble": "The open-mid back rounded vowel [ɔ] is raised to close-mid [o]. In Greek, Attic ω /ɔː/ merged with ο as /o/ once vowel length was lost (ὥρα /hɔ̌ː.raː/ > Koine /ˈo.ra/). In the second phase of the English Great Vowel Shift, Middle English /ɔː/ of boat was raised to /oː/ after 1600, later diphthongized to /oʊ/. Proto-Permic *ɔ became o in literary Komi (*bɔn > бон).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Vowel Raising, Merger",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Ancient Greek to Koine Greek",
      "languageFamily": "Hellenic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Attic ὥρα /hɔ̌ː.raː/ 'season, hour' (5th c. BCE)",
          "to": "Koine /ˈo.ra/ (4th c. CE), Modern Greek ώρα /ˈo.ra/"
        },
        {
          "from": "Attic δῶρον /dɔ̂ː.ron/ 'gift'",
          "to": "Koine /ˈðo.ron/, Modern Greek δώρο /ˈðo.ɾo/"
        },
        {
          "from": "Attic ὦμος /ɔ̂ː.mos/ 'shoulder'",
          "to": "Koine /ˈo.mos/, Modern Greek ώμος /ˈo.mos/",
          "note": "Wiktionary's 1st-century CE Egyptian Koine pronunciation already has /ˈo.mos/; ω and ο are no longer distinguished."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Late Middle English to Early Modern English",
      "languageFamily": "West Germanic",
      "examples": [
